<section class="block">
<h2>What launched</h2>
<p>ChatGPT Wallet lets OpenAI's agents complete purchases for users: people save a card, the agent shows the items, total and card before checkout, and the user approves each purchase; merchants and agents receive tokenised card credentials rather than the real card number<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. It is rolling out first to eligible users in the US, and OpenAI plans to support purchases within preset spending limits later<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Cards from Visa, Mastercard and American Express work at launch<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p>It lands on rails the card networks have been building: Visa and Mastercard launched agent payment programmes that issue tokens for approved agents and verify them at the network<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Reporting on the launch notes OpenAI warned that some sites may block agent purchases unintentionally and that fraud systems may flag legitimate agent orders<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How an agent purchase flows</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Intent</b><span>The user asks the agent to find and buy something</span></li>
<li><b>Browse</b><span>The agent reads product data and builds a basket</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Approve</b><span>The user sees items, total and card, and approves</span></li>
<li><b>Token</b><span>A network token is issued for this purchase only</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Checkout</b><span>The merchant receives the order and token; fraud checks run</span></li>
<li><b>After-sale</b><span>Returns, support and disputes stay with the merchant</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Two moments carry most of the risk: the approval the user sees and the fraud decision the merchant makes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>For merchants: four things to decide now</h2>
<p><strong>1. Welcome, tolerate or block agents.</strong> Bot protection tuned against scrapers will often block agent checkouts too. Decide your policy deliberately. If you want agent sales, learn to recognise verified agents, for example through signed agent requests, rather than opening the door to every bot.</p>
<p><strong>2. Tune fraud rules for a new pattern.</strong> Agent orders can look unusual: fast, consistent, from new devices, with tokens rather than typed card details. Review rules that would decline them wholesale, and watch approval rates for agent traffic separately from human traffic.</p>
<p><strong>3. Make product data agent-readable.</strong> Agents buy what your data says. Accurate structured data on price, stock, sizes, delivery and returns reduces wrong orders and disputes; see <a href="/insights/structured-data-for-ai-search/">structured data for AI search</a>.</p>
<p><strong>4. Plan for after the sale.</strong> Returns, customer service and disputes stay with you. Make sure support can see that an order came through an agent and what the customer approved, so arguments about "I did not mean to buy that" can be resolved.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>For anyone building agents that spend money</h2>
<p>The approval step is the most important design decision. A good confirmation shows exactly what will happen, in plain terms, inside the conversation, and needs a deliberate action to proceed. MCP's Apps extension shows the pattern for any agent action: an interactive card in the chat that the user confirms<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/ai-agents-can-pay-agentic-commerce/in-chat-confirmation.webp" alt="Chat interface where an agent shows an interactive card summarising an action, listing what it will change and offering a Confirm button for the user" width="1060" height="740" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>An in-chat confirmation card: the user sees exactly what the agent will do and confirms it. Payments need the same clarity, plus amount, merchant and card. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-11-21-mcp-apps/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol Blog, MCP Apps</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Before payment</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Clear summary<em>items, total, merchant, card</em></span><span>Deliberate approval<em>biometric or explicit confirm</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Limits</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Per-purchase and daily caps</span><span>Merchant and category allowlists</span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Credentials</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Single-use tokens<em>never the real card</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>After</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Receipt and audit trail</span><span>Easy cancel and dispute route</span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Layers for agents that spend money. Autonomy grows only as the lower layers prove themselves.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="callout"><p>An agent that can pay is an agent that can be tricked into paying. Keep a human approval on every purchase until limits, allowlists and monitoring have earned the right to remove it.</p></div>
<p>Prompt injection matters more once money moves: a product page or email that instructs the agent to buy something is an attack. Treat every page the agent reads as untrusted; see <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence for agents</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where this is heading</h2>
<p>Expect three things: agent traffic growing first in the US and then elsewhere as networks and wallets expand; pressure to allow purchases within limits without per-transaction approval; and regulators asking who is liable when an agent buys the wrong thing. Payment and consumer protection rules differ widely by market, so merchants selling internationally should watch how each region treats agent-initiated payments. See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>For merchants, the cost of preparing is small: a deliberate bot policy, fraud rules reviewed for agent traffic, clean product data and support tooling that shows how an order was made. For agent builders, the hard part is not the payment API but the approval experience and the limits around it. Both groups should start now, while volumes are low enough to learn. Read alongside <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-14/">the 14 November tech news</a>, <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent payments</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/agent-ready-travel-marketplace-sea/">agent-ready marketplace case study</a>.</p>
</section>

